# Git hooks are POSIX sh run by git's shell: they must check out with LF even on
# Windows (CRLF in a sh script is a syntax error), overriding core.autocrlf.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# The same reason, one directory over. `scripts/*.mjs` carry a `#!/usr/bin/env node`
# shebang AND are imported by the test suite as ESM: a CRLF shebang is "Invalid or
# unexpected token" to the test runner's transform, so the file fails to load rather
# than failing an assertion. Found 2026-09-08 — `src/lib/scoring/gate-cli.test.ts`
# could not load on Windows while CI (Linux, LF) was green, which is the worst shape
# a gate can take: unable to go green on the author's own machine, and therefore
# routed around rather than fixed.
scripts/*.mjs text eol=lf

# Prisma checksums each migration.sql byte-for-byte, and the migration tests read them with
# line-anchored regexes (`/TEXT;$/`): under core.autocrlf a Windows checkout turns every line end
# into CRLF, `$` then sits after a stray \r, and `loop-runs-arms.test.ts` fails while Linux CI is
# green. The index already holds LF; this makes the working tree match it.
prisma/migrations/**/migration.sql text eol=lf

# Vitest writes snapshots with LF. Under core.autocrlf a Windows checkout holds this one as CRLF, so
# every full suite run rewrote it back to LF and left the tree dirty. Pin the one file (the only
# .snap in the repo) to LF so the suite is a no-op on it.
src/lib/email/__snapshots__/digest-mail.test.ts.snap text eol=lf
